The war began when North Korea supported by China attacked South Korea in 1950 to unify the peninsula under its communist rule. However, many South Korean historians believe that their country was equally responsible for escalating the conflict by launching a surprise attack on North Korea in 1950 without provoking a formal declaration of war.

According to them, South Korea's military had been planning to launch a surprise attack on North Korea for several months and it finally did so on June 25, 1950, when its troops crossed the 38th parallel and attacked several key military targets in North Korea. The North Korean army responded swiftly and decisively to the attack and pushed the South Korean forces back across the border.

